From bf06efb4d95112ebb41305265c812b50debd256c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pugant <pugant@localhost>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 19:37:55 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 03/15] llama-context: restore the as-built topology when
 re-binding a cached graph [w6-9 a2-c2 rev-2]

The W6-9 cells crashed the server on the real VK path with the cache
enabled: ggml_abort in ggml_backend_sched_backend_id_from_cur during the
re-bind's split_graph ("pre-allocated tensor (attn_inp_kq_mask (copy)) in
a buffer (Vulkan0) that cannot run the operation (CPY)").

Root cause: a cached llm_graph_result holds the graph as its last
allocation left it, not as it was built. ggml_backend_sched_split_graph
mutates the user graph in place - it replaces node->src[j] with its
cross-backend copy tensors, created in sched->ctx, which split_graph
frees and re-initializes at every call, so those links dangle into
freed memory - and the vk graph optimizer reorders nodes[] through the
split views. The tensors also keep their gallocr-assigned buffer/data,
which ggml_gallocr_init_tensor does not re-assign (data != NULL skip)
and which backend_id_from_cur probes as pre-allocated (the abort). A
fresh build never sees any of this because freshly built tensors have
buffer == data == NULL and pristine links.

Fix: snapshot the as-built topology (nodes order + src links) right
after build_graph and before alloc_graph, and restore it (plus scrub
the allocation state of every tensor in the result's ggml context)
when a cache slot is re-bound. The re-bind then goes through exactly
the same split + alloc a fresh build would.

Also flip the default to legacy (k=1): the cache is strictly opt-in
via LLAMA_GRAPH_CACHE_SLOTS >= 2 until the cells re-pass - a default
must not be able to crash.

Reproduced in the builder container with zero GPU on lavapipe VK + CPU
(pre-fix: abort at the first re-bind; logs/wave6/w6c9/repro-prefix-k3.*),
fixed and verified bit-exact on both CPU-only and multi-backend arms.

Co-Authored-By: GLM by z.ai <glm@z.ai>
